const notes = {
  'hidden-fee': 'Use three passes. First, enter only charges printed in the offer so you have a conservative baseline. Second, add the charges that appear on a recent bill but were not in the original price. Third, run a high case for any fee that is described as variable, estimated, or subject to change. Keeping those cases separate prevents false precision and gives you a clean negotiation story: this is the advertised price, this is the documented fee load, and this is the exposure created by unclear language.',
  'contract-cost': 'The most useful output is the difference between a simple total and a fully loaded total. Save both numbers. Then ask which line items are fixed, which are usage-based, and which can change without a signed amendment. If you are comparing two vendors, use the same term, volume assumption, and escalation treatment for both. A consistent model exposes whether a lower quote is genuinely lower or simply leaves more of the cost outside the headline price.',
  'automatic-renewal': 'Put the result in two places: your calendar and your contract notes. Add the provider’s notice method, the recipient, and the exact agreement name to the reminder. If the renewal date is not obvious, treat that ambiguity as a review finding. A strong process starts before the deadline: obtain the current price, confirm the cancellation path, and decide whether you need a written confirmation that the renewal was stopped.',
  'price-escalation': 'Run a low, expected, and high scenario when the clause references an index or market rate. The low case can use a cap, the expected case can use recent inflation, and the high case can use the maximum permitted increase. Comparing the scenarios shows whether the clause creates manageable variance or an open-ended budget problem. Bring the spread to the negotiation instead of arguing about whether one percentage “sounds reasonable.”',
  'termination-fee': 'Model the exit cost alongside the cost of staying. Include any service value you would lose, replacement cost, transfer fee, or notice-period payment so the decision is not driven by one number. Then ask whether the clause contains exceptions for breach, nonperformance, relocation, or material change. A provider may be willing to waive a fee when the alternative is a documented service failure or a clean transfer to another customer.',
  'late-fee': 'Use the result as an invoice reconciliation note. Record the original due date, the date the provider says payment was received, the balance used as the base, and whether a grace period applied. If the late charge repeats, calculate each period separately rather than multiplying one amount blindly. That record makes it easier to challenge compounding, duplicate late charges, or a fee applied while the account was under a documented dispute.',
  'subscription-cost': 'Run the model at three checkpoints: the promotional period, the first renewal, and the end of the period you realistically expect to use the product. A discount that makes the first month attractive may disappear at renewal, while a setup fee may matter less over a long term. Add the cost of features you would otherwise buy separately. The right comparison is the useful service you receive, not the smallest number in the checkout flow.',
  'service-fee': 'Separate the fee base before you compare providers. One business may calculate a service fee on labor only; another may apply it to labor, materials, taxes, or the full invoice. Ask for the base in writing and keep a copy of the estimate. If the fee recurs after the service is complete, treat it as a different pricing component and model the timeline. That distinction can reveal a charge that is easy to miss because it shares the same label.',
  'processing-fee': 'Model transaction mix, not just an average. Small payments are affected more by fixed fees, while large payments are affected more by the percentage rate. Add separate scenarios for online, in-person, debit, rewards, and international transactions when the rate sheet distinguishes them. Then include statement, payout, equipment, chargeback, and minimum fees outside the transaction model. The effective rate is the total cost divided by the money processed.',
  'convenience-fee': 'Compare the surcharge with every payment option offered. If a fee is avoidable only by using a slower or less convenient channel, note that tradeoff rather than calling it optional automatically. Check whether the percentage is calculated before or after tax, whether the flat fee applies per payment, and whether the receipt names the charge consistently. A screenshot of the checkout page can be valuable if the final invoice later shows a different amount.',
  'contract-risk': 'Write one evidence note beside every score. Quote the heading, record the page, and explain the practical consequence. That discipline keeps the score from becoming a vague feeling about the agreement. Review high-scoring terms together because they can compound: a renewal clause matters more when price can rise at renewal, and a termination fee matters more when the provider controls notice or service scope. The score should lead to questions, not panic.',
  'hidden-fee-risk': 'A risk score is strongest when it is repeatable. Use the same definitions for every quote, and do not lower a score simply because a fee is familiar. Familiar charges can still be mandatory, uncapped, or charged more than once. Once you have the source document, replace assumptions with evidence: identify the fee, its base, timing, avoidability, and owner. A clear fee may remain expensive, but it is no longer hidden.',
  'invoice': 'Reconcile the invoice in layers: quantity times rate, subtotal, tax, fees, credits, and amount due. Compare each layer with the purchase order, estimate, contract, and prior invoice. If a line item is new, ask whether it is a new service or a new label for an existing charge. If a credit disappeared, ask when it was applied and where it is shown. A repeatable review process catches both arithmetic errors and scope drift.',
  'negotiation-savings': 'Use the estimate to rank asks by value and ease. Fee removal is often easier to approve than a permanent rate cut, while a cap or shorter renewal may protect more money over time. Build a written package: the requested concession, the reason, the start date, and the term. If the provider offers a credit, calculate its first-year value and compare it with the recurring savings you asked for. Keep the agreement itself as the source of truth.',
  'consumer-savings': 'Build the estimate from statements, not memory. Look back over several months so you catch annual fees, renewal charges, and seasonal services. Group the opportunities into cancel, switch, correct, and negotiate. That keeps the savings plan actionable. After each change, check the next statement and record the actual reduction. The difference between estimated and realized savings is useful feedback: it shows which fee categories deserve closer document analysis next time.'
};

